Structure: 
	- Imports: import { XXX } from '@jupyterlab/yyy';
	- needs to export a variable extension (dict-like) that has id, requires and activate
	- requires tells all the requirements that are passed to activate
	- Signature of activate method has to match the requirements, additionally first parameter is jupyterlab app itself (application shell, needed e.g. for creating tabs and registering new commands)
	- Main part is in activate (program entry point), can do anything there, mostly connecting to Phosphor signals to which I can then react because only very few stuff needs to be executed only once on start, most stuff needed when certain events occur (and thus signals are sent)

The API is a bit different, finally found API documentation: http://jupyterlab.github.io/jupyterlab

Uses Phosphor everywhere, need to connect to signals instead of registering events

NotebookActions provides information (sends signal) about codecell execution, CodeConsole for console

